The Human-English works in a complete lattice, with bottom the supremum of the empty set and top the supremum of the whole lattice, and defines \(x \ll y\) to mean that \(y\) lies in the interior, for the induced topology, of the principal upset of \(x\). The elementary features are [Proposition 2.2](1972_Proposition_2_2.md). Scott gives the relation no name; the notes call it way-below, following later usage. `WayBelow` is that relation, witnessed by a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(y\) contained in the principal upset of \(x\), and the infix \(\ll\) is notation for it. The seven clause lemmas live in `WayBelow.lean`. `proposition_2_2` is Scott's statement, and its proof calls those lemmas. `bot_wayBelow` proves (i) because the universe is a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(x\) and is contained in the upset of bottom. `WayBelow.sup` proves (ii) by intersecting the two witnessing neighbourhoods, so a point of the intersection lies above both \(x\) and \(y\) and hence above their join. `WayBelow.trans_le` proves (iii) because a Scott-open set is an upper set, so the neighbourhood of \(y\) already contains \(z\). `WayBelow.le_trans` proves (iv) because a neighbourhood contained in the upset of \(y\) is contained in the upset of \(x\). `WayBelow.le` proves (v) because \(y\) itself lies in the witnessing neighbourhood. `WayBelow.self_iff_scottOpen_Ici` proves (vi): if \(x \ll x\), a witnessing neighbourhood contained in the principal upset equals that upset, since the neighbourhood is an upper set containing \(x\), and conversely a Scott-open principal upset is a neighbourhood of \(x\) contained in itself. `WayBelow.sSup_iff` proves (vii) for a nonempty directed set, matching the paper’s convention that directed sets are nonempty: a witnessing neighbourhood of the supremum meets the set, and the same neighbourhood witnesses \(x \ll s\) at the meeting point, while the converse is monotonicity on the right because the member lies below the supremum. Interpolation and the continuous-lattice equation are [Continuous lattices](1972_ContinuousLattice.md). `scottOpen_wayBelow` proves that \(\{z \mid x \ll z\}\) is Scott-open, which is not one of the seven clauses: a witnessing neighbourhood of the supremum of a directed set meets that set, and the same neighbourhood witnesses the relation at the meeting point. `directedOn_wayBelow` proves that \(\{x \mid x \ll y\}\) is directed in any complete lattice, because the join of two such elements is again way below \(y\) by Proposition 2.2(ii). `sInf_wayBelow` proves that the infimum of a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(y\) is way below \(y\), because that neighbourhood contains \(y\) and is contained in the principal upset of its infimum. **Theorem:** yes — the interior definition and Proposition 2.2 are formalized. **Proof:** partial — the definition is a definition, and clauses (i)–(vii) together with the openness, directedness, and neighbourhood-infimum facts are proved.
